  "question": "Can a target be scored from structure in a way that does not penalise the interfaces macrocycles exist to bind?",
  "answer": "Yes. Scoring the largest contiguous exposed hydrophobic patch, rather than pocket depth or enclosure, ranks the spike receptor-binding domain above the main protease -- the reverse of a small-molecule druggability score, and the right way round for this project.",
  "why_not_a_druggability_score": "fpocket, SiteMap and similar are calibrated on small-molecule sites: deep, enclosed, buried. A macrocyclic peptide buries 400-800 square angstroms against a large flat surface. Scoring pocket depth would reproduce the enzyme bias the keyword rubric already has, through geometry instead of vocabulary.",
